  {
    "episode_id": 81498,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Bill, one of the things that if I, you know, talk to people in East Palestine and Fran, I've been over here. I think six or seven times now. One of the things that people said to me was, look, I feel okay, but who am I going to be in a year, two years, three years? You know, I lived within, you know, half a mile of where this train derailment occurred. And so what became clear to me is that we do have some people who have had symptoms or maybe in some cases still have symptoms, they certainly need medical care, they need to talk with the doctor. But we also have people who don't have any symptoms, but who say, look, I want to make sure that I'm covered if in five years something happens to me as a result of, you know, I get cancer or something happens as a result of this train wreck. So we wanted to make sure that we had a full functioning clinic right in East Palestine. that people could go in and, you know, get a kind of a benchmark where they are today. So if something happens, you know, in five years, then, you know, they could demonstrate where they were at this point and a place where people could go back every year if they wanted to and get checked. But also, frankly, this is a community that lacks primary care. and does not have enough primary care. So we established today, Fran and I are over here today to officially announce and actually open a new clinic. This is a clinic that is in conjunction with Ohio Department of Health and East Liverpool Hospital and it's going to be a permit a permanent clinic, and people will be able to come in here. They're starting to take walk-ins today. Actually, one person as we were talking came in instead of an appointment. And this is going to be located in a small building that used to be a primary care physician's office. And he retired a few years ago, I guess. And so we're going to go right back into that office and people will feel comfortable about coming in there. This is a community of about 47, 4,800, but we anticipate that people from the surrounding area will also come in as well. I mean, I think not only will this help deal with people's concerns as a result of the train wreck But also it's going to, I think, improve people's health in general because of access to a primary care physician. One of the problems we have in Ohio and other states is in our rural areas, many times people are not physically that close to a primary care physician.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
